course: dictionary
lesson: 1913
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and «combination» is one of them. The sentence around the word, its context, tells you which meaning to use. A combination can be two or more things mixed together, like a combination of colours, or the numbers that open a safe.
explain: Read the clues. In «Knowing the parts lets you crack new words like a code, but not every combination is a real word, so check new ones in a dictionary», the combination means letters joined together to make a new word. Nothing in the sentence is about locks or numbers, so that meaning does not fit. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Maths uses the word in a special way. A combination is one way of choosing items from a group when the order does not matter. Three tops and two skirts make 3 × 2 = 6 outfits, so there are 6 combinations of top and skirt.
fact: A combination is two or more things joined or mixed together.
fact: A combination can also be the set of numbers needed to open a combination lock or safe.
fact: In maths, a combination is one way of choosing items from a group where the order does not matter.
fact: In science, chemicals in combination make new substances.
fact: Useful phrases: «in combination with», «a winning combination», «combination lock».
exercise: «Not every combination is a real word, so check new ones in a dictionary.» What does combination mean in this sentence? | A pair of shoes | A kind of cake | A large party | * Letters joined together
exercise: A combination lock opens only when you set the right what? | Sounds | Keys | Colours | * Numbers
exercise: A shop has 3 tops and 2 skirts. How many outfits can be made? | * 6 | 32 | 1 | 5
test: What does «in combination with» mean? | Far away from | * Working together with | Exactly the same as | Stuck to
test: In maths, when does the order not matter in a combination? | When you are spelling a word | When you are timing a race | When you are counting in a line | * When you are choosing items from a group
exam: Read «a combination of colours» and say which meaning is used. | * Two or more things mixed together | The act of marking a test | One way of choosing items from a group | The numbers that open a safe
